A||N||4||Will||decreasing turning circle||||1||
Z||000000||Will||05-02-2001||04:49 PM||||I did the beretta brake thing to the front of my car last weekend. When I was sandblasting and painting the caliper mount brackets, I noticed that they had a finger on them that acted as a steering stop. I turned the wheel all the way without the bracket on, and it looks like I could cut 1/4-3/8" off the bracket and be able to steer the front wheels further, tightening up the turning radius.<P>That's one thing that's annoyed me about the early suspension: for having such a short wheelbase, the car has a much larger turning circle than it should.||||reg||1
Z||000001||rockcrawl||05-02-2001||05:17 PM||||Was that on an '84 or an '85-'87? The '84 had boxed lower control arms and suposedly had a larger turning radius because of it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tryxalon||05-02-2001||05:38 PM||||Wondered about that myself. <P>Anyone done this? Why wouldn't it work?? <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Steve Normington||05-02-2001||06:59 PM||||On my 85 SE V-6, if the wheels turned any more, the caliper mount would hit the shock absorber.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jimmy||05-02-2001||07:03 PM||||Will, I think Ed Parks has even done this.  I think it was on P.Zurich list a few years ago I read a post from him about cutting the stops off of the frame.  You have to make sure you just don't cut them too far down.<BR>Haven't done this myself.<P>Jim||||reg||1
